WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.790
Marcus Swanson, Filmmaker and LA Film School
Student: You spoke in the beginning about

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:08.710
the rapid change in the industry causing problems.
I wanted to know if you think students should

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:14.970
try to keep up-to-date with things that they’ve
previously learned if by the time they graduate

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.119
it will already be kind of evolved?

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:21.749
Houston Howard, Transmedia Author and Instructor:
One hundred percent. Everything changes so

00:00:21.749 --> 00:00:30.009
fast that now it’s incumbent upon creators
not just students but professionals especially

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:38.129
professionals that have been in the industry
awhile. The typical 30-year screenwriter that

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:44.160
has seen and done it all they find it difficult
to really want to stay on top of things.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:51.359
I think students naturally because of the
generation they have grown up multi platform

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:55.730
and they’ve naturally learned all of the
new platforms and learn all the new stuff.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:06.350
I think now we live in such a crazy, oversaturated,
technologically advanced time that really

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:15.040
if you want to stay relevant you need to figure
out how to utilize and maximize and leverage

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:21.210
the new platform, the new touch points and
methodologies that audiences are using every

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:27.690
single day and it’s not necessarily because
the theater is going away or Netflix is ensuring

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:32.399
that everything streams. It’s not necessarily
that. I always put it in reference to the

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:38.310
audience. If you understand your audience
and you can design to your audience then you

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:44.530
are going to be a better creator. And if you
start to see audiences operating on multi-platforms,

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:50.680
if you see audiences really gravitating into
podcasts. Podcasts are very big, they are

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:57.470
very popular today whereas two or three years
ago not as much. But if you see a big push

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:03.270
into podcasts then all of the sudden that
should clue you in that there is an opportunity

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.119
to the platform but in the mindset of the
audience.

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:13.040
Once you start to understand that audience
you will be able to take that psychology and

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.700
lace it through how you are going to create
your IP [intellectual property].

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:25.150
I think a lot of the new platforms with mobile,
with podcasts, with like Alexa, Google Home,

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:30.209
all of that stuff. I think understanding how
that is starting to shift culture. Why are

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:34.070
people starting to use voice? Why is voice
coming back? It’s almost like we are coming

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.099
back to a 1930’s radio era.

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:48.070
I think that instead of being threatened by
that that creators, screenwriters, directors,

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:56.330
authors they should recognized that and leverage
that as a tool into those things.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.790
What I typically get is is “Well I don’t
know how to make a podcast?” “I don’t

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:05.319
know how to make an Alex skill. I don’t
know how to do any of that stuff.” Like

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:12.690
you can’t teach old dogs new tricks. I just
don’t believe that. I tell people there

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:17.970
is this amazing website that teaches you how
to do anything in the world and it’s called

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.761
G - O - O - G - L - E [dot] com. You go there
and type in “How do I make a podcast? How

00:03:22.761 --> 00:03:34.129
do I create an Alexa Skill? How do I do something
on IGTV? And you can spend 7 or 9 or 48 hours

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:39.459
reading and learning how to do all of this
stuff and now all of the sudden you have an

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:51.090
extra skill on your Batman belt as a screenwriter
not necessarily to augment it in a way that

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.170
is flowing in line with culture and that I
think is flowing in line with culture and

00:03:54.170 --> 00:04:00.329
that I think is empowering rather than threatening.
I’m a big advocate of never stop learning

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:04.599
especially as a creator because if you never
stop learning you are always going to be in

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:11.790
line with your audience.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:17.820
How do we differentiate between transmedia
as a marketing strategy compared to like a

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:24.640
storytelling strategy? Should I be using transmedia
in my storytelling or is it just a marketing

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.420
ploy?

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:36.180
Houston: Historically bad transmedia has been
rooted in marketing and what happened and

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:41.680
what happened (nothing against marketing)
marking is not a bad term, it’s not a pejorative

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:50.820
term but where transmedia breaks down is when
creators push transmedia off to the marketing

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:57.690
people and really don’t understand the story
or they don’t have the right to extend the

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:07.300
story. So a lot of times you will see a marketing
heavy transmedia pushes that are light on

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:13.520
story and are never about just trying to exploit
the media and create and entry point for the

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.790
audience and there isn’t a valuable extension
of the story.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:25.480
For me the best transmedia flows through the
creator into all these touch points where

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:38.900
now the mobile game and the Facebook live
and the IGTV series they are not just marketing

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:44.970
tools, they are storytelling tools and the
big difference is how much story are you getting?

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.630
Is it story first or is it just promotional
first?

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:58.500
The more promotional it is the less valuable
it is to the audience. I always liken it to

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.750
me and my wife have two dogs. We love our
dogs…we are like weird dog people that love

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:11.320
our dogs too much. And so I always use the
analogy that you need to feed your audience

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:17.880
and feed your fans like you feed your dogs.
That is the first lesson that you learn as

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:23.060
a kid with a dog is that you need to feed
your dog every day, it’s your responsibility

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.370
(that’s what my dad told me). He said if
we’re getting a dog, you are going to take

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:31.900
care of it, it’s your responsibility to
feed it every single day. And creators need

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:36.580
to have that same attitude when it comes to
their fans. It’s their responsibility to

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:42.790
continue to feed the fans. Don’t put it
off onto a marketing person, to a publicist

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:48.460
or to other people (to PAs or interns). You
need to understand this as a creator how do

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:54.330
I feed my audience? Which then begs the question
what’s the best food to feed the audience?

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.500
The best food to feed the audience is story.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:05.271
As a GAME OF THRONES fan the best thing you
can feed me is a GAME OF THRONES story. Not

00:07:05.271 --> 00:07:10.890
necessarily a GAME OF THRONES trailer or commercial.
I’m excited about those things but they

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:16.760
are like sugar rushes. So if you eat a bunch
of sugar, if you feed you dog just a bunch

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:21.311
of junk, if you feed it a treat, it may get
excited but that’s not going to nourish

00:07:21.311 --> 00:07:23.580
them over the long term.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:31.780
So the thing that nourishes the audience the
most is just honest to God just good story.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:39.800
If you tell me as a GAME OF THRONES fan that
there is great story delivered on Instagram

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:45.550
I don’t care that that is Instagram, I go
there for the story. And I consume that story

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:50.630
and now there is a brand equity that continues
to strengthen in me through the GAME OF THRONES

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:56.490
brand in general because they fed me and the
more you feed you audience the more loyal

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:57.490
they are.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:02.040
Because if you don’t feed your dog what
happens? They either die, they go somewhere

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:08.140
else for food, they turn on you and eat you
like Ramsey Bolton (that’s a GAME OF THRONES

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:17.210
reference). But anyway you cut it or your
neighbor who has the food lures them away.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:22.610
So now in an oversaturated market you have
all these people saying “Hey I’ll feed

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.490
you over here! I’ll feed you over here!”
And now there’s competition in the marketplace.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:35.020
So for me the thing that differentiates marketing
and transmedia is what kind of food are you

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:42.289
feeding your audience? Is it story or is it
just promotional. And if the creator is involved

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:47.681
and it’s part of the creative vision, it’s
more likely than not be more story than it

00:08:47.681 --> 00:08:52.600
is promotional. As soon as the creators disconnects
and hands it off to the digital marketing

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:56.710
people they may call it transmedia but it’s
probably just going to be a big marketing

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:56.720
plot.

